# Deploying FoodHub to Google Cloud (Cloud Run)

This deploys the backend API and the frontend as two separate Cloud Run services. Both are containerized with the Dockerfiles already included in `backend/` and `frontend/`.

**Storage caveat first:** the backend stores data in a local `db.json` file. Cloud Run containers are stateless — the filesystem resets on redeploys, and if the service ever scales to more than one instance, each instance has its own copy of the data. For a demo/portfolio deploy this is fine (see the "keep it stable" note in step 3). For anything real, swap `lowdb`/`db.json` for Firestore or Cloud SQL before going further.

## Prerequisites

1. A Google Cloud project with billing enabled.
2. [Install the gcloud CLI](https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/install), then:
   ```bash
   gcloud auth login
   gcloud config set project YOUR_PROJECT_ID
   ```
3. Enable the required APIs (one-time per project):
   ```bash
   gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com cloudbuild.googleapis.com artifactregistry.googleapis.com
   ```

You don't need Docker installed locally — `gcloud run deploy --source` builds the container for you in the cloud using the Dockerfile in each folder.

## Step 1 — Deploy the backend

```bash
cd backend

gcloud run deploy foodhub-api \
  --source . \
  --region us-central1 \
  --allow-unauthenticated \
  --set-env-vars JWT_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32),JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d
```

- `--allow-unauthenticated` makes the API publicly reachable (needed so the frontend can call it from the browser).
- The `openssl rand -hex 32` generates a real random secret instead of the placeholder in `.env.example` — don't reuse that placeholder in production.
- When it finishes, gcloud prints a **Service URL**, e.g. `https://foodhub-api-abc123-uc.a.run.app`. Copy it — you'll need it in step 2.

Verify it's live:
```bash
curl https://foodhub-api-abc123-uc.a.run.app/api/health
# {"status":"ok"}
```

## Step 2 — Point the frontend at the backend

Create `frontend/.env.production` with the URL from step 1 (include `/api`):

```bash
cd ../frontend
echo "REACT_APP_API_URL=https://foodhub-api-abc123-uc.a.run.app/api" > .env.production
```

Create React App bakes `REACT_APP_*` variables into the build at build time, so this file must exist **before** the next step.

## Step 3 — Deploy the frontend

```bash
gcloud run deploy foodhub-frontend \
  --source . \
  --region us-central1 \
  --allow-unauthenticated
```

This builds the React app and serves it via nginx (see `frontend/Dockerfile` and `frontend/nginx.conf`). Cloud Run prints a Service URL when it's done — that's your live app.

## Step 4 — Test it

Open the frontend URL in a browser. Log in with the seeded demo admin:

- **Email:** `admin@foodhub.com`
- **Password:** `admin123`

Or register a new customer account and place a test order.

## Keeping the demo data stable

Because of the file-storage caveat above, if you want the seeded/admin-added data to stay consistent between requests, pin the backend to a single instance:

```bash
gcloud run services update foodhub-api \
  --region us-central1 \
  --min-instances 1 \
  --max-instances 1
```

This avoids Cloud Run spinning up a second instance with a fresh copy of `db.json`. It also means the service stays warm (no cold starts) but will incur small ongoing cost instead of scaling to zero.

## Redeploying after changes

Whenever you edit backend or frontend code, just re-run the same `gcloud run deploy` command from the matching folder — it rebuilds and creates a new revision automatically:

```bash
cd backend  && gcloud run deploy foodhub-api --source . --region us-central1
cd ../frontend && gcloud run deploy foodhub-frontend --source . --region us-central1
```

## Custom domain (optional)

```bash
gcloud run domain-mappings create \
  --service foodhub-frontend \
  --domain www.yourdomain.com \
  --region us-central1
```

Follow the DNS records Google gives you to verify ownership and point your domain at the service.

## Troubleshooting

- **Frontend loads but API calls fail / CORS errors:** double check `frontend/.env.production` has the correct backend URL (including `/api`) and that you rebuilt (`gcloud run deploy`) after changing it.
- **"Permission denied" invoking the service:** make sure you included `--allow-unauthenticated` on both services.
- **Login works but data disappears after a while:** see the min/max instance pinning note above, or migrate off `db.json` to a real database.
- **Build fails on `npm ci`:** make sure `package-lock.json` is committed/included — `npm ci` requires it (it's already in this project).
